From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 05:28:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] proc: a browser isn't started where there is no display to
 show it on (#5958)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A sign-in on a VPS was seen to call the server's own browser, which
came up and went away, and the Google authorization went unfinished —
the user's browser is on another machine (#5958). magpie web itself
opens nothing there (its OpenURL is a no-op since #102; the page opens
sign-in links itself), and the community agy-cli plugin's login runs
agy's own terminal sign-in, printing the link and taking the code
back, opening no browser at all. But the surfaces that do open a
browser all go through proc.OpenBrowser, which started the launcher
wherever magpie runs: `magpie plugin login agy-cli` over SSH started a
browser with nothing to show it on and said nothing, since only the
launcher failing to start was an error, and `magpie web` on a server
did the same with the page's own link.

So OpenBrowser refuses where there is nothing to open a browser on:
the xdg-open family with neither DISPLAY nor WAYLAND_DISPLAY set gets
an error instead of a browser that can only start and die, and the
caller shows it with the link it already prints, for a browser on the
machine the user is at. WSL is spared: its xdg-open is wslview, which
opens Windows' own browser with no Linux display, as magpie there
always has (#2043 came from one). darwin's open, Windows' rundll32 and
termux-open-url all reach a screen of their own without a display
here, and stay as they are. The desktop window's fallback sits behind
a Wails window, so no no-display machine runs it.

Run:
- TestBrowserWithoutDisplayStartsNoneAndSaysWhy: WSL starts its
  browser with no display, a server with neither variable set starts
  none and gets "there is no display on this machine to open a browser
  on", a DISPLAY or a WAYLAND_DISPLAY starts one again, and darwin,
  Windows and Android keep theirs (red before the fix: the seam the
  tests drive didn't exist — the compile was the red).
- TestInWSL: WSL1's and WSL2's kernels read as microsoft; a generic
  one and an unreadable one don't.
- Ablation: the guard's condition held false — the no-display case
  started a browser and gave no error ("no display: err <nil>, started
  2") — restored, green.
- TestBrowserNamesRundll32ByItsPath, the whole proc package, -race on
  the new tests, the gui sign-in open tests
  (TestPluginSignInPageOpensOnce, TestSignInLinkOnlyOpensNothing,
  TestMCPSignInSaysTheBrowserDidntOpen, TestOpenSaysTheBrowserDidntOpen,
  TestSignInAlertOpensItsProvider, TestSignInText) and a nogui build.

Not tried: a real headless Linux (none here) — the fixtures stand in
for the display's variables and the kernel's name.

